---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Yorozu — Your Mac's AI agents, from your iPhone" description="Yorozu lets you talk to OpenClaw, Claude Code and Codex on your Mac from your iPhone, end-to-end encrypted through a relay that cannot read your messages.">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script is:inline src="/copy.js" defer></script>
    <meta property="og:title" content="Yorozu">
    <meta property="og:description" content="Your Mac's AI agents, from your iPhone. End-to-end encrypted.">
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://yorozu.yumi.to/">
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://yorozu.yumi.to/assets/icon.png">
  </Fragment>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="title">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1 id="title">Your Mac's agents.<br><em>In your pocket.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">Yorozu <span class="kana" lang="ja">(よろず)</span> puts the AI agents on your own Mac (OpenClaw, Claude Code and Codex) on your iPhone. Start a task on the train, approve a command from the lock screen, read the result at your desk.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href="/mac">Download for Mac</a>
          <a class="button ghost" href="/iphone">Join iPhone TestFlight</a>
        </div>
        <p class="fine">Mac: macOS 15+ · Apple silicon · <a href="https://openclaw.ai">OpenClaw</a> on the host<br>iPhone: iOS 18+ · public beta · <a href="#setup-with-an-agent">Set up with an agent</a> · <a href="https://github.com/izyuumi/yorozu">Open source, MIT</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="phone">
        <div class="iphone-device">
          <img class="iphone-screen" src="/assets/iphone-chat.webp" alt="Yorozu on iPhone 18 Pro planning a Moon Picnic Club outing, with a snack budget and an emergency-cookie request" width="804" height="1748" fetchpriority="high">
          <img class="iphone-bezel" src="/assets/iphone-18-pro-bezel.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="1350" height="2760">
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="agent-setup" id="setup-with-an-agent" aria-labelledby="agent-setup-title">
    <div class="agent-setup-text">
      <h2 id="agent-setup-title">Set it up with one prompt.</h2>
      <p>Paste this into Claude Code, Codex or any coding agent on your Mac. It checks what you already have, then walks you through installing both apps, permissions, OpenClaw with its Yorozu channel, and pairing your iPhone. It asks before every change and never decides for you.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prompt">
      <pre><code id="agent-prompt">Read https://raw.githubusercontent.com/izyuumi/yorozu/main/Start.md and follow it to set up Yorozu on my devices. Ask me before configuring anything.</code></pre>
      <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="agent-prompt" hidden>Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p class="copy-status" id="agent-prompt-status" role="status" aria-atomic="true"></p>
    <p class="fine">The guide is <a href="https://github.com/izyuumi/yorozu/blob/main/Start.md">Start.md</a> in the repository. Prefer to do it yourself? <a href="#iphone-availability">Install</a> and <a href="#pair">pair</a> by hand. Either way, read <a href="#before-you-install">Before you install</a> first.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="proof" aria-labelledby="approve-title">
    <div class="proof-text">
      <h2 id="approve-title">A yes or no, from wherever you are.</h2>
      <p>When an agent wants to run a command, send a message or spend money, the question lands on your phone as a card. Quick decisions get <strong>Allow</strong> and <strong>Don't allow</strong> right on the notification. Anything bigger opens the card, so nothing dangerous is one accidental tap away.</p>
      <p>Say <em>Discuss first</em> and the agent explains before it acts.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot phone-shot">
      <div class="iphone-device">
        <img class="iphone-screen" src="/assets/iphone-approval.webp" alt="Yorozu on iPhone 18 Pro asking permission to send a playful Moon Picnic Club invitation, with Allow once, For this task, Don't allow and Discuss first buttons" width="804" height="1748" loading="lazy">
        <img class="iphone-bezel" src="/assets/iphone-18-pro-bezel.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="1350" height="2760" loading="lazy">
      </div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="proof flip" aria-labelledby="mac-title">
    <div class="proof-text">
      <h2 id="mac-title">Your Mac does the work.</h2>
      <p>OpenClaw answers by default, with Yorozu as one of its chat channels and the providers, models, tools and memory you configured there. Its scheduled jobs can post into a Yorozu thread too. Open a thread as <strong>Claude Code</strong> or <strong>Codex</strong> instead and it runs in a folder under <code>~/Projects</code>, with that agent's own permissions.</p>
      <p>Threads sync between the Mac and every paired device. Pick up on the desktop exactly where the phone left off.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot mac-shot">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/assets/mac-demo-dark.webp" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" width="820" height="540">
        <img src="/assets/mac-demo-light.webp" alt="Yorozu on a Mac desktop with demo threads and a playful Saturday adventure plan, including a park stroll and an emergency snack fund" width="820" height="540" loading="lazy">
      </picture>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="more" aria-labelledby="more-title">
    <h2 id="more-title">Made for a phone in one hand.</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>Share from any app</dt><dd>Send a link, some text or a photo from the share sheet straight into a thread.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>See what needs you</dt><dd>The thread list shows which agents are running, done, or waiting on your answer.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Search everything</dt><dd>Search the full history on your Mac, not just what your phone has loaded.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>More than one Mac</dt><dd>Pair several Macs and see all their threads in one list.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="proof duo-support" id="duo" aria-labelledby="duo-title">
    <div class="proof-text">
      <h2 id="duo-title">Also supports iPhone Duo.</h2>
      <p>Open your iPhone Duo to see your sessions and conversation side by side.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot duo-shot">
      <div class="duo-device">
        <picture>
          <source srcset="/assets/iphone-duo-dark.webp" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" width="1440" height="1013">
          <img src="/assets/iphone-duo-light.webp" alt="Yorozu on iPhone Duo fully open in landscape, with demo sessions on the left, a dinner-planning conversation on the right, and New session at the lower right" width="1440" height="1013" loading="lazy">
        </picture>
        <img class="duo-bezel" src="/assets/iphone-duo-bezel.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="3093" height="2247" loading="lazy">
      </div>
      <figcaption class="fine">iPhone Duo, fully open in landscape.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="relay" aria-labelledby="relay-title">
    <h2 id="relay-title">The relay can't read a word of it.</h2>
    <figure class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="Your iPhone and your Mac exchange messages encrypted with ChaCha20-Poly1305 through a relay that sees only size and timing.">
      <span class="diagram-art" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <figcaption aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="diagram-encryption">ChaCha20-Poly1305 · End-to-end encrypted</span>
        <span class="diagram-labels"><span>iPhone</span><span class="diagram-relay">sees size and timing</span><span>Mac</span></span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>Keys never leave your devices</dt><dd>An X25519 exchange at pairing time. The relay stores public keys and a room id, nothing it could open.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>No Yorozu account</dt><dd>No email, no password, no analytics. Your provider logins stay in OpenClaw, Claude Code and Codex on the Mac.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Run it yourself if you like</dt><dd>The relay is a few hundred lines. Put it on a Mac mini over Tailscale and change one URL in Settings.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="iphone-availability" id="iphone-availability" aria-labelledby="iphone-title">
    <div class="availability-intro">
      <h2 id="iphone-title">Get the apps.</h2>
      <p>The Mac app is free and updates itself. The iPhone app is in public beta on TestFlight; there is no App Store release yet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="installation-options">
      <article>
        <h3>Mac</h3>
        <p>Download the app, move it to Applications and open it. Yorozu lives in your menu bar. Want early builds? <a href="/beta">Download the beta</a> and turn on beta updates in Settings.</p>
        <a href="/mac">Download for Mac</a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>iPhone, via TestFlight</h3>
        <p>Open the link on your iPhone, install <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/testflight/id899247664">TestFlight</a> if prompted, then install Yorozu. Beta builds update automatically.</p>
        <a href="/iphone">Join on TestFlight</a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>iPhone, from source</h3>
        <p>Developers can use Xcode and Tuist on a Mac. Follow the development guide to generate the workspace, then open <code>apps/ios/Yorozu.xcworkspace</code> in Xcode. For a physical iPhone, configure your own signing team and provisioning for the app and its extensions, select the <code>YorozuIOS</code> scheme and your connected iPhone, then run.</p>
        <a href="https://github.com/izyuumi/yorozu#development">Open the development guide</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="steps" id="pair" aria-labelledby="steps-title">
    <h2 id="steps-title">Then pair once.</h2>
    <p class="steps-intro">You’ll need an Apple silicon Mac with macOS 15 or later and <a href="https://docs.openclaw.ai/install">OpenClaw</a> configured. Keep your host Mac awake and online to reach it from your phone.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
        <h3>Set up your host Mac</h3>
        <p>Open Yorozu from the menu bar, choose “Host Yorozu on this Mac” and grant the permissions you want. Then add Yorozu to OpenClaw as a chat channel with <a href="https://github.com/izyuumi/yorozu#install">four commands</a>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        <h3>Scan one code</h3>
        <p>In Mac Settings → Devices, choose “Pair Another Device”. Open Yorozu on your iPhone and scan the code. No Yorozu account needed.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
        <h3>Talk from anywhere</h3>
        <p>Every thread lives on the Mac. Your phone, and any other Mac, just reaches it.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="warning" id="before-you-install" aria-labelledby="risk-title">
    <h2 id="risk-title">Before you install</h2>
    <p><strong>Using Yorozu could get your Claude or ChatGPT/Codex account suspended or banned.</strong> Yorozu drives Claude Code and Codex on your Mac with the account you signed in with. Anthropic and OpenAI decide what use of their subscriptions is allowed, change those rules without notice, and have suspended accounts used with third-party agent tools. If you want the lowest risk, use an API key rather than a subscription login.</p>
    <p>AI agents take real actions on your Mac: they run commands, edit and delete files, and can send messages. Review what you approve. See the <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
